{
  "_comment": [
    "Measured visibility baseline for the theme background effects.",
    "",
    "HOW THESE NUMBERS WERE PRODUCED. scripts/verify/measure-theme-effect-visibility.py",
    "drives a real Chromium at 1280x800, device_scale_factor 1, applies each theme",
    "through the real registry, waits for the effect to accumulate frames, then",
    "screenshots the page twice: once with the effects canvas display:none and once",
    "with it visible. measuredMaxDelta is the largest absolute per-channel RGB",
    "difference between those two plates, in 0-255. It is what a human can see.",
    "",
    "noiseFloorMaxDelta is a control: two screenshots taken back to back with the",
    "canvas hidden both times. It is 0 for every theme, which is what makes the",
    "measured delta trustworthy rather than a reading of some other animation.",
    "",
    "decorativeAlpha is the dependency-free proxy computed by tests/helpers/ink-probe.mjs.",
    "It is the MINIMUM peak over 24 independent inits, not a single sample. Effects",
    "reseed from Math.random() on every init, and codex in particular ranges 0.0196",
    "to 0.0307 run to run; recording one sample made the floor test flaky. For a",
    "floor, the worst case the effect actually produces is the honest statistic.",
    "It does NOT linearly predict measuredMaxDelta across themes - snes composites",
    "It does NOT linearly predict measuredMaxDelta across themes - snes composites",
    "13/255 from an alpha of 0.0104 while corporate_v2 needed 0.12 to reach 9/255,",
    "because coverage, tint contrast and backdrop luminance all move the result.",
    "That is exactly why the test asserts each theme against ITS OWN recorded",
    "value instead of one global alpha floor: a single floor failed codex, hermes",
    "and snes, all three of which are plainly visible in a browser.",
    "",
    "REGENERATE after changing any effect's intensity, and look at the screenshots",
    "the script writes before committing the new numbers. A baseline nobody looked",
    "at is just a number that agrees with itself."
  ],
